\title{Transition Endomorphisms: A Chapman--Kolmogorov Law\\ and the Stabilization of Transition Rank}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{2026-06-20}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
We introduce the \emph{transition endomorphism} of a stream of linear
self-maps on a vector space: given a sequence $f : \N \to \End_K(V)$ and
indices $i \le j$, the transition endomorphism $\trans(f, i, j)$ is the
ordered composition $f(j-1) \circ \cdots \circ f(i)$ over the half-open
window $[i, j)$, with the convention that an empty or reversed window
yields the identity. Our central structural result is a \emph{concatenation
law} of Chapman--Kolmogorov type: for $i \le j \le k$,
$\trans(f, i, k) = \trans(f, j, k) \circ \trans(f, i, j)$. From this single
identity, combined with the classical submultiplicativity of rank under
composition, we derive that the transition rank is monotone non-increasing
along nested windows. We identify the constant-stream transition map with
the monoid power $g^{\,j-i}$, which yields the rank decay of iterates
$\rank(g^{\,n+1}) \le \rank(g^{\,n})$. Finally, over a finite-dimensional
space we package the cardinal rank as a bounded natural-number sequence and
prove that the window-from-zero transition-rank sequence is antitone and
hence eventually constant, via a reusable well-foundedness lemma for
antitone $\N \to \N$ sequences. The development deliberately reuses
standard rank facts rather than re-deriving a Sylvester-type inequality.
All results have been formally verified.
\end{abstract}

\maketitle

\section{Introduction}

A recurring pattern across mathematics is the \emph{transfer operator}: an
object that advances a system one step at a time, and whose iterated or
windowed composition advances it over an interval. In probability this is
the transition kernel of a Markov chain; in dynamics it is the
Perron--Frobenius/Koopman operator; in linear time-varying control it is
the state-transition matrix. In each case a bookkeeping law holds---to
advance from $i$ to $k$, advance from $i$ to $j$ and then from $j$ to
$k$---and a notion of ``surviving information'' can only degrade as the
window grows.

This paper isolates that pattern in its purest linear-algebraic form. We
work with a stream of endomorphisms of a vector space, define the
\emph{transition endomorphism} over a window, prove the windowed
composition law, and show that essentially every rank-monotonicity
phenomenon is a one-line corollary of it given the standard
submultiplicativity of rank under composition.

\paragraph{Contributions.}
\begin{enumerate}
  \item A recursive definition of the transition endomorphism
    $\trans(f, i, j)$ and its boundary behaviour (Section~\ref{sec:def}).
  \item The Chapman--Kolmogorov concatenation law (\texttt{transEndo\_comp},
    Theorem~\ref{thm:comp}).
  \item Rank monotonicity along nested windows
    (\texttt{rank\_transEndo\_le\_left}, \texttt{rank\_transEndo\_le\_right},
    \texttt{rank\_transEndo\_succ\_le}, \texttt{rank\_transEndo\_antitone})
    (Section~\ref{sec:rank}).
  \item Identification of constant streams with monoid powers
    (\texttt{transEndo\_const}) and the consequent rank decay of iterates
    (\texttt{rank\_pow\_succ\_le}) (Section~\ref{sec:const}).
  \item Eventual constancy of the natural-number transition-rank sequence
    over a finite-dimensional space (\texttt{rankSeq\_le\_finrank},
    \texttt{rankSeq\_antitone}, \texttt{rankSeq\_eventually\_const}), via
    the reusable order lemma \texttt{antitone\_nat\_eventually\_const}
    (Section~\ref{sec:stab}).
\end{enumerate}

A design principle throughout is \emph{reuse over re-derivation}: we treat
the submultiplicativity inequalities as black boxes and let the
combinatorial concatenation law carry the structural weight.

\section{Preliminaries and notation}

Throughout, $K$ is a field, $V$ a vector space over $K$, and $\End_K(V)$
the unital ring of $K$-linear self-maps $V \to V$ under composition, with
identity $\id$. We write $g \circ h$ for composition and $g^{\,n}$ for the
$n$-fold composite ($g^{\,0} = \id$). The \emph{rank} of
$g \in \End_K(V)$ is $\rank(g) = \dim_K \im(g)$, a cardinal in general and
a natural number when $V$ is finite-dimensional; in that case we write
$n = \dim_K V$.

We take as given the two classical \emph{submultiplicativity}
inequalities, for all $g, h \in \End_K(V)$:
\begin{align}
\rank(g \circ h) &\le \rank(g), \tag{S-left}\label{eq:sleft}\\
\rank(g \circ h) &\le \rank(h). \tag{S-right}\label{eq:sright}
\end{align}
\eqref{eq:sleft} holds since $\im(g \circ h) \subseteq \im(g)$, and
\eqref{eq:sright} since $g|_{\im(h)}$ has image $\im(g \circ h)$. These are
the only nontrivial external facts we use; in particular we do not re-prove
any Sylvester rank inequality.

A sequence $a : \N \to \N$ is \emph{antitone} if
$m \le m' \Rightarrow a(m') \le a(m)$, and \emph{eventually constant} if
$a(m) = c$ for some $c$ and all $m \ge N$.

\section{The transition endomorphism}\label{sec:def}

\begin{definition}[Transition endomorphism]\label{def:trans}
Let $f : \N \to \End_K(V)$ and $i \in \N$. The \emph{transition
endomorphism} is defined by recursion on the upper endpoint:
\[
\trans(f, i, 0) = \id,
\qquad
\trans(f, i, j+1) =
\begin{cases}
f(j) \circ \trans(f, i, j) & \text{if } i \le j,\\
\id & \text{if } i > j.
\end{cases}
\]
For $i \le j$ this unfolds to the ordered composition over $[i, j)$,
\[
\trans(f, i, j) = f(j-1) \circ f(j-2) \circ \cdots \circ f(i),
\]
read right-to-left. The formal name is \texttt{transEndo f i j}.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Basic identities]\label{lem:basic}
We have:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textup{(\texttt{transEndo\_zero})} $\trans(f, i, 0) = \id$;
  \item \textup{(\texttt{transEndo\_succ\_of\_le})} if $i \le j$ then
    $\trans(f, i, j+1) = f(j) \circ \trans(f, i, j)$;
  \item \textup{(\texttt{transEndo\_eq\_id\_of\_le})} if $j \le i$ then
    $\trans(f, i, j) = \id$;
  \item \textup{(\texttt{transEndo\_self})} $\trans(f, i, i) = \id$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
(1) and (2) are definitional. For (3): if $j = 0$ it is the base case; if
$j = j'+1$ then $j \le i$ forces $i > j'$, so the recursion takes the
identity branch. (4) is the case $j = i$ of (3).
\end{proof}

\section{The concatenation law}\label{sec:comp}

\begin{theorem}[Chapman--Kolmogorov concatenation,
\texttt{transEndo\_comp}]\label{thm:comp}
For all $i \le j \le k$,
\[
\trans(f, i, k) = \trans(f, j, k) \circ \trans(f, i, j).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Induct on $k$, generalizing $i$ and $j$.

\emph{Base $k = 0$.} Then $i = j = 0$ and both sides equal $\id$.

\emph{Step $k \mapsto k+1$.} Assume the law for $k$ and let
$i \le j \le k+1$.

\emph{Case $j \le k$.} Then $i \le k$. By
Lemma~\ref{lem:basic}(2),
$\trans(f, i, k+1) = f(k) \circ \trans(f, i, k)$ and
$\trans(f, j, k+1) = f(k) \circ \trans(f, j, k)$. Apply the induction
hypothesis to $\trans(f, i, k)$ and reassociate:
\[
f(k) \circ \big(\trans(f, j, k) \circ \trans(f, i, j)\big)
= \big(f(k) \circ \trans(f, j, k)\big) \circ \trans(f, i, j)
= \trans(f, j, k+1) \circ \trans(f, i, j).
\]

\emph{Case $j = k+1$.} Then $\trans(f, j, k+1) = \trans(f, k+1, k+1) = \id$
by Lemma~\ref{lem:basic}(4), so the right-hand side equals
$\trans(f, i, k+1)$, the left-hand side. This case is where the empty-window
convention is used, so the theorem is not vacuous.
\end{proof}

\section{Rank monotonicity}\label{sec:rank}

\begin{theorem}[Outer-factor bound,
\texttt{rank\_transEndo\_le\_left}]\label{thm:left}
For $i \le j \le k$, $\rank(\trans(f, i, k)) \le \rank(\trans(f, j, k))$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Theorem~\ref{thm:comp},
$\trans(f, i, k) = \trans(f, j, k) \circ \trans(f, i, j)$; apply
\eqref{eq:sleft}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Inner-factor bound,
\texttt{rank\_transEndo\_le\_right}]\label{thm:right}
For $i \le j \le k$, $\rank(\trans(f, i, k)) \le \rank(\trans(f, i, j))$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Same factorization; apply \eqref{eq:sright}.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[One-step decrease,
\texttt{rank\_transEndo\_succ\_le}]\label{cor:succ}
For $i \le j$, $\rank(\trans(f, i, j+1)) \le \rank(\trans(f, i, j))$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
Theorem~\ref{thm:right} with $k = j+1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Antitonicity,
\texttt{rank\_transEndo\_antitone}]\label{cor:anti}
For $i \le j \le k$, $\rank(\trans(f, i, k)) \le \rank(\trans(f, i, j))$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
Restatement of Theorem~\ref{thm:right}.
\end{proof}

\section{Constant streams and iterate rank}\label{sec:const}

\begin{theorem}[Constant stream, \texttt{transEndo\_const}]\label{thm:const}
If $f(n) = g$ for all $n$, then for $i \le j$,
$\trans(f, i, j) = g^{\,j-i}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Induct on the witness of $i \le j$. Base $j = i$:
$\trans(f, i, i) = \id = g^{0}$. Step from $j$ to $j+1$ with $i \le j$:
using $g^{(j-i)+1} = g \circ g^{\,j-i}$ and Lemma~\ref{lem:basic}(2),
\[
\trans(f, i, j+1) = g \circ \trans(f, i, j) = g \circ g^{\,j-i}
= g^{(j+1)-i}.\qedhere
\]
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Iterate rank decay,
\texttt{rank\_pow\_succ\_le}]\label{thm:pow}
For every $g \in \End_K(V)$ and $n \in \N$,
$\rank(g^{\,n+1}) \le \rank(g^{\,n})$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Write $g^{\,n+1} = g \circ g^{\,n}$ and apply \eqref{eq:sright}.
Equivalently, specialize Corollary~\ref{cor:anti} via
Theorem~\ref{thm:const}.
\end{proof}

\section{Stabilization of the transition-rank sequence}\label{sec:stab}

Assume $V$ finite-dimensional, $n = \dim_K V < \infty$, so $\rank(g)$ is a
natural number $\le n$.

\begin{definition}[Transition-rank sequence]\label{def:seq}
For $i \le j$, set $\rankseq(f, i, j) = \big(\rank \trans(f, i, j)\big)_\N$,
the natural-number value of the finite cardinal rank (\texttt{rankSeq f i j}).
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Uniform bound, \texttt{rankSeq\_le\_finrank}]\label{lem:bound}
For all $i \le j$, $\rankseq(f, i, j) \le n$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
The image of any endomorphism is a subspace of $V$, of dimension $\le n$;
transfer to $\N$ since the rank is below $\aleph_0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Antitonicity in $\N$, \texttt{rankSeq\_antitone}]\label{lem:antiN}
For fixed $i$ and $i \le j \le k$,
$\rankseq(f, i, k) \le \rankseq(f, i, j)$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Apply the order-preserving $\operatorname{toNat}$ (valid for finite
cardinals) to Corollary~\ref{cor:anti}.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Well-foundedness,
\texttt{antitone\_nat\_eventually\_const}]\label{lem:wf}
Every antitone $a : \N \to \N$ is eventually constant.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
The value set $\{a(m)\} \subseteq \N$ has a least element $c = a(N)$. For
$m \ge N$, antitonicity gives $a(m) \le c$ and minimality gives
$a(m) \ge c$, so $a(m) = c$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Eventual constancy,
\texttt{rankSeq\_eventually\_const}]\label{thm:evconst}
For every stream $f$ over a finite-dimensional $V$, the sequence
$m \mapsto \rankseq(f, 0, m)$ is eventually constant.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Lemma~\ref{lem:antiN} (with $i = 0$) the sequence is antitone; by
Lemma~\ref{lem:wf} it is eventually constant. Lemma~\ref{lem:bound}
confirms the bound $\le n$.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
The eventual value is the \emph{stable transition rank} of the stream. For
$f(n) = g$, Theorem~\ref{thm:const} identifies the sequence with
$m \mapsto \rank(g^{\,m})$, whose stable value is
$\dim_K \big(\bigcap_m \im(g^{\,m})\big)$, the Fitting (generalized) image.
\end{remark}

\section{Algorithms}

\paragraph{Windowed transition rank.} Given $M_0, \dots, M_{T-1} \in
K^{n\times n}$ and a window $[i, j)$, compute $\trans(f, i, j)$ by the
recursion of Definition~\ref{def:trans} (left-multiplying by $M_j$ as $j$
advances) and report its rank by Gaussian elimination. Cost
$O\big((j-i)\,n^{\omega}\big)$.

\paragraph{Detecting stabilization.} Compute the antitone sequence
$r_m = \rankseq(f, 0, m)$ incrementally. By Theorem~\ref{thm:evconst} it is
non-increasing in $[0, n]$ and strictly drops at most $n$ times; halt once
the value has provably stabilized. Each step costs $O(n^\omega)$.

\section{Applications}

\paragraph{Linear time-varying systems.} The state-transition matrix from
$i$ to $k$ of $x_{t+1} = A_t x_t$ is $\trans(A, i, k)$;
Theorem~\ref{thm:comp} is the semigroup property and Corollary~\ref{cor:anti}
quantifies the loss of reachable dimension.

\paragraph{Markov chains (analogy).} With stochastic matrices,
Theorem~\ref{thm:comp} is Chapman--Kolmogorov.

\paragraph{Operator iteration.} Theorem~\ref{thm:const} reduces $g^{\,m}$ to
the constant-stream transition map, recovering the descending iterate-image
chain underlying the Fitting decomposition.

\section{Discussion}

A well-chosen combinatorial law can subsume a cluster of analytic-looking
inequalities. Once Theorem~\ref{thm:comp} is in hand, the rank statements
are each one application of a standard submultiplicativity fact; we never
re-prove a Sylvester inequality. Two boundary conventions do real work: the
identity on empty windows (used in the $j=k+1$ case of
Theorem~\ref{thm:comp}) and recursion on the upper endpoint (aligning the
step lemma with window growth).

\section{Future work}

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \emph{Sharp eventual-rank floor:} for a constant stream the eventual
    value of $\rankseq(f, 0, \cdot)$ equals
    $\dim_K \big(\bigcap_m \im(g^{\,m})\big)$.
  \item \emph{Stabilization by index $n$:} $\rankseq(f, 0, m) = \rankseq(f, 0,
    n)$ for all $m \ge n$.
  \item \emph{Sub-window superadditivity:} for $i \le j \le k$,
    $\rankseq(f, i, k) \ge \rankseq(f, i, j) + \rankseq(f, j, k) - n$.
  \item \emph{Invertible streams:} if every $f(n)$ is an automorphism then
    $\rankseq(f, i, j) = n$ for all $i \le j$.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Conclusion}

We have built a small, self-contained theory of transition endomorphisms on
a stream of linear self-maps. A single Chapman--Kolmogorov concatenation law
organizes the development: rank monotonicity along nested windows, the
identification of constant streams with monoid powers, the rank decay of
iterates, and the eventual constancy of the transition-rank sequence in
finite dimension. The transition endomorphism serves as a reusable transfer
operator whose rank is automatically antitone along nested windows and
stabilizes in finite dimension.

